WEBVTT

1
00:00:39.297 --> 00:00:42.540
<i>Buenos Aires is growing
uncontrollably and imperfectly.</i>

2
00:00:43.035 --> 00:00:45.948
<i>An overpopulated city
in a deserted country.</i>

3
00:00:46.171 --> 00:00:50.210
<i>A city in which thousands
of buildings rise into the sky.</i>

4
00:00:50.575 --> 00:00:52.316
<i>Arbitrarily.</i>

5
00:01:06.625 --> 00:01:09.265
<i>Next to a tall one, a small one.</i>

6
00:01:09.561 --> 00:01:12.735
<i>Next to a rational one,
an irrational one.</i>

7
00:01:13.565 --> 00:01:17.206
<i>Next to a French one,
one with no style at all.</i>

8
00:01:18.236 --> 00:01:21.979
<i>These irregularities
probably reflect us perfectly.</i>

9
00:01:22.674 --> 00:01:25.712
Aesthetic and ethical
irregularities.

10
00:01:37.055 --> 00:01:40.036
<i>These buildings,
which adhere to no logic,</i>

11
00:01:40.158 --> 00:01:43.037
<i>represent bad planning.</i>

12
00:01:45.664 --> 00:01:47.507
<i>Just like our lives:</i>

13
00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:53.547
<i>We have no idea how
we want them to be.</i>

14
00:01:56.741 --> 00:02:00.188
<i>We live as if Buenos Aires
were a stopover.</i>

15
00:02:00.679 --> 00:02:03.819
<i>We've created
a "culture of tenants".</i>

16
00:02:28.306 --> 00:02:33.722
<i>The buildings are becoming smaller
to make space for even smaller ones.</i>

17
00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:40.521
<i>Apartments are measured
by their number of rooms</i>

18
00:02:41.353 --> 00:02:44.857
<i>and range from five rooms
with balconies,</i>

19
00:02:45.023 --> 00:02:48.527
<i>playrooms, servants' quarters
and storerooms</i>

20
00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.533
<i>to one-room apartments
also known as "shoeboxes".</i>

21
00:03:03.275 --> 00:03:06.347
<i>Just like almost
all man-made objects,</i>

22
00:03:06.478 --> 00:03:10.153
buildings are made
to differentiate between us.

23
00:03:12.751 --> 00:03:17.894
<i>There's a front and a back side.
High and low apartments.</i>

24
00:03:18.423 --> 00:03:22.872
<i>Privileged people have the letter A
or sometimes B.</i>

25
00:03:23.094 --> 00:03:27.008
<i>The farther back in the alphabet,
the worse the apartment.</i>

26
00:03:27.966 --> 00:03:32.540
<i>The promised view and brightness
rarely coincide with reality.</i>

27
00:03:35.807 --> 00:03:39.721
<i>What can be expected of a city
that turns its back on its river?</i>

28
00:03:41.079 --> 00:03:44.117
<i>I'm convinced that separations,
divorces</i>

29
00:03:44.449 --> 00:03:48.420
<i>domestic violence,
the excess of cable TV stations,</i>

30
00:03:48.553 --> 00:03:51.932
<i>the lack of communication,
listlessness,</i>

31
00:03:52.057 --> 00:03:56.199
<i>apathy, depression, suicide,</i>

32
00:03:56.361 --> 00:03:58.967
<i>neuroses, panic attacks,</i>

33
00:03:59.164 --> 00:04:01.838
obesity, tenseness,

34
00:04:02.067 --> 00:04:04.479
<i>insecurity, hypochondria,</i>

35
00:04:04.636 --> 00:04:07.446
<i>stress and a sedentary lifestyle</i>

36
00:04:07.572 --> 00:04:11.918
<i>are attributable to architects
and builders.</i>

37
00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:16.889
<i>I suffer from all of these illnesses
except suicide.</i>

38
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.470
<i>This is my one-room apartment.
400 square feet</i>

39
00:04:38.603 --> 00:04:42.016
<i>and one miserable window
for one breathless lung.</i>

40
00:04:42.707 --> 00:04:45.711
<i>Santa Fé, 1105.</i>

41
00:04:45.877 --> 00:04:48.619
<i>Fourth ?oor. H.</i>

42
00:04:55.587 --> 00:04:57.794
<i>I sat down at my computer
ten years ago</i>

43
00:04:57.922 --> 00:05:01.062
<i>and feel like
I've never got up again.</i>

44
00:05:01.359 --> 00:05:04.670
<i>I don't know if the Internet is
the future, but it was mine:</i>

45
00:05:04.796 --> 00:05:08.300
<i>I design websites
and this is my cyberspace</i>

46
00:05:08.433 --> 00:05:10.640
<i>I don't know if I'm good
or just got into it early,</i>

47
00:05:10.769 --> 00:05:12.146
<i>but I'm very busy.</i>

48
00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:15.717
<i>I began with my psychiatrist’s
website for phobics:</i>

49
00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.845
<i>His specialty and the reason
why I visit him twice a week.</i>

50
00:05:28.019 --> 00:05:29.965
<i>This game is a hit.</i>

51
00:05:30.088 --> 00:05:34.230
<i>It's for insomniacs
fighting pill addiction.</i>

52
00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:42.303
<i>My psychiatrist calls me
a phobic on my way to recovery.</i>

53
00:05:42.434 --> 00:05:46.075
<i>Due to repeated, violent panic attacks,
I locked myself in my apartment</i>

54
00:05:46.371 --> 00:05:47.645
<i>for years.</i>

55
00:05:48.606 --> 00:05:51.985
<i>I won 17 championships
at expert level.</i>

56
00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:54.090
<i>Four times undefeated,
nine times top scorer.</i>

57
00:05:54.813 --> 00:05:57.293
<i>I beat Federer four times
at Wimbledon.</i>

58
00:05:58.416 --> 00:06:01.659
<i>I became Godfather
of the Corleone family</i>

59
00:06:01.786 --> 00:06:04.562
<i>I was totally isolated. Scared.</i>

60
00:06:04.756 --> 00:06:08.863
<i>My psychiatrist developed
a strategy to help me overcome</i>

61
00:06:08.993 --> 00:06:11.769
<i>my fear of the city:
Photography.</i>

62
00:06:12.464 --> 00:06:14.876
<i>A way to rediscover
the city and people.</i>

63
00:06:16.768 --> 00:06:19.942
<i>A search for beauty where
it's not apparent.</i>

64
00:06:20.071 --> 00:06:22.608
<i>Observing is being and not being.</i>

65
00:06:22.741 --> 00:06:25.620
<i>Or being in another way.</i>

66
00:06:26.378 --> 00:06:29.120
<i>I distracted myself.</i>

67
00:06:29.581 --> 00:06:33.996
<i>I don't take the bus or taxis.
Much less the subway,</i>

68
00:06:34.119 --> 00:06:38.067
<i>and I'd never take a plane.
I only go by foot</i>

69
00:06:38.223 --> 00:06:42.262
<i>and always have
my survival backpack.</i>

70
00:06:42.394 --> 00:06:46.342
<i>Contents: a Leica D-Lux 3
with 10 mega pixels.</i>

71
00:06:46.498 --> 00:06:50.344
<i>Rivotril drops, 2.5 mg.
Amoxicillin 500.</i>

72
00:06:50.468 --> 00:06:54.507
<i>Ibuprofen. Sunglasses.
A plastic rain coat.</i>

73
00:06:54.639 --> 00:06:57.142
<i>A Victorinox with 21 tools.</i>

74
00:06:57.308 --> 00:07:01.757
<i>Flashlight with batteries.
Condoms, three units.</i>

75
00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:06.590
<i>400 pesos cash in small bills.
An iPod with 60 GB</i>

76
00:07:06.718 --> 00:07:09.096
<i>and more than 8,000 songs.</i>

77
00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:12.758
<i>Three Tati films. A notebook.
And a plastic card</i>

78
00:07:12.891 --> 00:07:16.202
<i>about how to proceed
in case of an accident</i>

79
00:07:16.327 --> 00:07:17.499
<i>or panic attack.</i>

80
00:07:25.136 --> 00:07:30.085
<i>The backpack weighs 5.8 kG.
7 % of my body weight.</i>

81
00:07:41.419 --> 00:07:45.492
<i>I've been an architect for two years,
but still haven't built anything.</i>

82
00:07:45.623 --> 00:07:50.197
<i>Neither a building, nor a house,
nor a bathroom. Nothing.</i>

83
00:07:50.361 --> 00:07:54.639
<i>Only models that are uninhabitable,
and not only because of their scale...</i>

84
00:07:56.234 --> 00:07:59.181
<i>I didn't fare well
with other constructions either:</i>

85
00:07:59.337 --> 00:08:01.408
<i>My four-year relationship collapsed</i>

86
00:08:01.539 --> 00:08:04.315
<i>despite my ef fons to shore it up.</i>

87
00:08:04.476 --> 00:08:07.650
<i>If my life were a game of life,</i>

88
00:08:07.812 --> 00:08:12.192
<i>I'd have to move back five spaces.</i>

89
00:08:12.650 --> 00:08:14.425
<i>That's why I'm here:</i>

90
00:08:14.552 --> 00:08:17.726
<i>With my disorderly life in 27 boxes,</i>

91
00:08:17.856 --> 00:08:21.895
<i>I sit on twelve meters
of bubble wrap, popping bubbles</i>

92
00:08:22.026 --> 00:08:24.666
<i>so that I myself don't pop.</i>

93
00:08:37.442 --> 00:08:40.013
<i>This is my new old shoebox</i>

94
00:08:40.144 --> 00:08:43.819
<i>that these ridiculous five steps
make into a "duplex".</i>

95
00:08:45.683 --> 00:08:49.460
<i>And this is the freakish thing,
half window, half balcony,</i>

96
00:08:49.587 --> 00:08:52.227
<i>that the sun shuns all year long.</i>

97
00:08:55.093 --> 00:08:59.838
<i>Avenida Santa Fe, 1183.
Eighth floor. G</i>

98
00:09:00.031 --> 00:09:01.339
<i>as in gastritis.</i>

99
00:09:04.202 --> 00:09:07.979
<i>This is my favourite building
in Buenos Aires.</i>

100
00:09:08.139 --> 00:09:11.211
<i>It's the best location
and the most fun.</i>

101
00:09:11.676 --> 00:09:14.282
<i>It's built with
my favourite materials:</i>

102
00:09:14.412 --> 00:09:17.086
Concrete, steel and glass.

103
00:09:17.782 --> 00:09:21.491
<i>One of the world's few buildings</i>

104
00:09:21.619 --> 00:09:24.099
<i>with an equilateral
triangular base,</i>

105
00:09:24.222 --> 00:09:27.362
<i>it was inspired
by Saturn and its rings.</i>

106
00:09:27.692 --> 00:09:30.798
<i>Even if most people
see a flying saucer.</i>

107
00:09:31.729 --> 00:09:36.439
<i>I enter it expecting
to take off and leave this world.</i>

108
00:09:42.674 --> 00:09:46.781
<i>But in reality,
the planetarium puts me in my place,</i>

109
00:09:46.911 --> 00:09:50.222
<i>reminding me that
the world doesn't revolve around me.</i>

110
00:09:50.348 --> 00:09:53.625
<i>I'm a very small
pan of a planet</i>

111
00:09:53.751 --> 00:09:58.359
<i>that's pan of a system
that's pan of a galaxy</i>

112
00:09:58.623 --> 00:10:03.163
<i>that, like hundreds of thousands
of galaxies, forms pan of the universe.</i>

113
00:10:04.462 --> 00:10:09.935
<i>It reminds me that I'm pan of
an infinite and eternal whole.</i>

114
00:10:11.469 --> 00:10:15.007
A SHORT AUTUMN

115
00:11:16.968 --> 00:11:18.948
The dog committed suicide.

116
00:11:19.871 --> 00:11:25.446
Apparently it was a 40-year-old
prostitute's only companion

117
00:11:25.743 --> 00:11:30.954
and was locked out on the balcony
so it wouldn't bother her customers.

118
00:11:33.351 --> 00:11:36.355
The dog went mad when
they touched her.

119
00:11:39.991 --> 00:11:42.301
No wonder it jumped

120
00:11:42.827 --> 00:11:46.798
Alone, on such a small balcony.

121
00:11:47.999 --> 00:11:49.910
STRANGE ACCIDENT
IN BUENOS AIRES:

122
00:11:50.034 --> 00:11:53.140
A DEAD DOG
AND TWO INJURED PEOPLE

123
00:12:23.768 --> 00:12:25.679
Sig nature.

124
00:12:28.573 --> 00:12:30.519
Thanks.

125
00:14:53.384 --> 00:14:56.422
DISC ARTHROSIS

126
00:15:02.326 --> 00:15:06.297
You look terrible,
but you're perfectly healthy.

127
00:15:06.664 --> 00:15:11.545
The only strange things are the fifth and sixth
vertebrae, which you've had since birth.

128
00:15:11.669 --> 00:15:17.984
Otherwise, you'd be screaming
in pain, so it's not that.

129
00:15:18.409 --> 00:15:21.413
Are you dizzy? No.

130
00:15:21.979 --> 00:15:26.553
Just do some fitness training
and lighten up your backpack.

131
00:15:28.252 --> 00:15:30.789
There's nothing serious,
nothing at all.

132
00:15:32.156 --> 00:15:34.158
You see...

133
00:15:36.193 --> 00:15:39.402
Those reports are written by kids

134
00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:42.773
in laboratories or hospitals
who write

135
00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:45.107
down everything to protect
themselves.

136
00:15:45.236 --> 00:15:48.843
They're not perfect.
What can you do?

137
00:15:49.106 --> 00:15:52.315
If you want to worry, go ahead,
but not about that.

138
00:16:13.731 --> 00:16:15.711
You're the same.

139
00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:39.361
<i>Until I can work as an architect,
I'm designing shop windows.</i>

140
00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:42.594
<i>It distracts me
from other thoughts.</i>

141
00:16:44.495 --> 00:16:47.408
<i>I think of the windows
as lost places.</i>

142
00:16:47.531 --> 00:16:50.569
<i>They're neither inside
nor outside.</i>

143
00:16:51.135 --> 00:16:53.342
<i>An abstract and magic space.</i>

144
00:16:53.971 --> 00:16:55.973
<i>They reflect a pan of me.</i>

145
00:16:56.107 --> 00:16:58.747
<i>At the same time,
the anonymity calms me.</i>

146
00:16:59.210 --> 00:17:02.214
<i>Maybe it's stupid, burl think:</i>

147
00:17:02.346 --> 00:17:04.485
<i>If someone stops to look,</i>

148
00:17:04.615 --> 00:17:07.425
<i>they're somehow interested in me.</i>

149
00:17:45.923 --> 00:17:48.631
<i>I've had this book since I was 14.</i>

150
00:17:48.759 --> 00:17:51.399
<i>And forgive me, great writers,</i>

151
00:17:51.529 --> 00:17:54.066
<i>but it's a key book in my life.</i>

152
00:17:54.365 --> 00:17:57.107
<i>It's the origin of
my fear of crowds,</i>

153
00:17:57.234 --> 00:18:00.875
<i>which has become existential.</i>

154
00:18:02.006 --> 00:18:04.077
<i>It dramatically represents</i>

155
00:18:04.208 --> 00:18:08.953
<i>the fear of knowing
I'm one lost person among millions.</i>

156
00:18:10.014 --> 00:18:14.326
<i>Years have passed and I still can't
solve one of the puzzles:</i>

157
00:18:14.552 --> 00:18:16.759
<i>"Wally in the City".</i>

158
00:18:16.887 --> 00:18:21.199
<i>I found him shopping,
at the airport and the beach,</i>

159
00:18:21.325 --> 00:18:23.828
but not in the city.

160
00:18:23.994 --> 00:18:27.703
<i>Perhaps my nerves
have blinded me.</i>

161
00:18:28.299 --> 00:18:30.404
<i>So I'm wondering:</i>

162
00:18:30.534 --> 00:18:34.641
<i>If I can't find a person
when I know who I'm looking for,</i>

163
00:18:34.772 --> 00:18:38.845
<i>how can I find a person when
I don't know who I'm looking for?</i>

164
00:18:40.377 --> 00:18:44.154
"Hi, I'm Susْ,
part stuffed animal and part dog."

165
00:18:44.281 --> 00:18:46.784
<i>Seven years ago, my girlfriend
visited her parents</i>

166
00:18:46.917 --> 00:18:52.128
<i>in New Jersey, USA.
She wanted to stay for a few weeks.</i>

167
00:18:52.389 --> 00:18:55.768
<i>As painful as it was for her,
she called me collect to say</i>

168
00:18:55.893 --> 00:18:58.134
<i>she wasn't coming back.</i>

169
00:18:58.295 --> 00:19:02.072
<i>It was her new home and she'd
realised she was very American.</i>

170
00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:07.979
<i>Ingenious! She felt very American
when Argentina was devalued.</i>

171
00:19:08.105 --> 00:19:13.248
<i>The truth is, there wasn't much
to keep her here: me and her dog.</i>

172
00:19:13.444 --> 00:19:16.857
<i>She left her with the best
of intentions too.</i>

173
00:19:16.981 --> 00:19:19.791
<i>She claimed the change in language</i>

174
00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:21.861
<i>would be too difficult for her.</i>

175
00:19:36.667 --> 00:19:39.079
You know planes make me nervous.

176
00:19:39.203 --> 00:19:42.116
Please take the rock I travel with.

177
00:19:42.239 --> 00:19:46.085
- You've only travelled once.
- Well, it worked.

178
00:19:46.977 --> 00:19:50.515
It's my kabbalah. Do it for me.

179
00:19:53.551 --> 00:19:55.326
Alright.

180
00:20:04.395 --> 00:20:08.275
<i>AH at once,
I lost the woman I loved</i>

181
00:20:08.499 --> 00:20:10.945
<i>and my ability to fly.</i>

182
00:20:47.271 --> 00:20:51.777
<i>Hello, this is Mariana.
Leave a message after the beep.</i>

183
00:20:57.615 --> 00:20:59.686
<i>Hello, hello. Are you there?</i>

184
00:21:03.387 --> 00:21:07.301
<i>Alright. I just wanted
to tell you that tonight</i>

185
00:21:07.758 --> 00:21:11.205
<i>a great film will be on TV</i>

186
00:21:11.562 --> 00:21:13.872
<i>with Bill Murray
and the groundhog.</i>

187
00:21:15.165 --> 00:21:19.705
<i>And because you took the TV,
I thought we could watch it together.</i>

188
00:21:21.205 --> 00:21:24.618
<i>Pick up. I know you're there.</i>

189
00:21:30.014 --> 00:21:33.826
<i>Are you with a guy?
You screwed me!</i>

190
00:21:33.951 --> 00:21:37.694
<i>How can you be close
to someone so different?</i>

191
00:21:38.222 --> 00:21:43.103
<i>That's the stupid conclusion
after a four-year relationship.</i>

192
00:21:44.995 --> 00:21:47.635
<i>Four years are 48 months.</i>

193
00:21:47.798 --> 00:21:50.972
<i>1460 days.</i>

194
00:21:51.302 --> 00:21:55.717
<i>35,040 hours
with the wrong person.</i>

195
00:21:59.109 --> 00:22:04.149
<i>I watched him one night
and realised everything:</i>

196
00:22:04.982 --> 00:22:07.724
<i>For the first time, he was distant,</i>

197
00:22:07.851 --> 00:22:11.458
<i>as if he were completely unfamiliar.</i>

198
00:22:11.822 --> 00:22:16.066
<i>I suddenly feared
being alone with a stranger.</i>

199
00:22:19.430 --> 00:22:20.875
<i>And here I am:</i>

200
00:22:20.998 --> 00:22:24.707
In the same apartment
that I abandoned to live with him.

201
00:22:24.968 --> 00:22:29.713
<i>In front of the same mirror.
Four years later.</i>

202
00:23:17.454 --> 00:23:21.561
A LONG WINTER

203
00:24:07.104 --> 00:24:08.674
Thanks.

204
00:24:55.319 --> 00:24:58.493
<i>The Internet brings me closer to
the world, but further from life.</i>

205
00:24:58.622 --> 00:25:01.626
<i>I do banking and read magazines
on the Internet,</i>

206
00:25:01.859 --> 00:25:04.362
<i>download music and listen to
the radio on the Internet,</i>

207
00:25:04.761 --> 00:25:08.368
<i>order food on the Internet,
watch films on the Internet,</i>

208
00:25:08.532 --> 00:25:11.012
<i>chat on the Internet,
study on the Internet,</i>

209
00:25:11.268 --> 00:25:16.377
<i>play on the Internet, have sex
on the Internet and search for...</i>

210
00:25:16.507 --> 00:25:17.884
DOG SITTER

211
00:25:21.311 --> 00:25:24.520
Come here, Susْ.

212
00:25:25.749 --> 00:25:29.663
Come here, Susْ. Come.

213
00:25:43.333 --> 00:25:45.939
- This is Susْ.
- Alright.

214
00:25:46.703 --> 00:25:49.479
She's not used to
being with other animals.

215
00:25:49.606 --> 00:25:53.816
- Then you'd better come with us.
- Alright, I'll take her.

216
00:26:07.724 --> 00:26:11.399
- Can you get me a fag from my pocket?
- Here?

217
00:26:19.069 --> 00:26:21.345
- Got a light?
- No.

218
00:26:22.873 --> 00:26:25.877
- Get it from my pocket.
- Here?

219
00:27:32.275 --> 00:27:33.879
- How long?
- Are you gay?

220
00:27:39.416 --> 00:27:42.761
- Because of the dog
- Oh, right.

221
00:27:45.389 --> 00:27:50.737
It's not mine. It belongs to a friend
who went to the USA.

222
00:27:50.861 --> 00:27:52.499
She asked me to take care of it.

223
00:27:52.629 --> 00:27:55.303
She had a problem
and couldn't come back,

224
00:27:55.432 --> 00:27:57.537
sol kept it.

225
00:27:57.701 --> 00:28:02.446
And since she left,
it's... it's more depressed,

226
00:28:02.606 --> 00:28:06.076
it doesn't bark, it's insecure

227
00:28:07.310 --> 00:28:10.018
and doesn't want to leave the house.
So I thought it'd be good

228
00:28:10.147 --> 00:28:13.890
for it to meet other dogs and people.

229
00:28:16.086 --> 00:28:18.896
I think she's adapting.
What do you think?

230
00:28:19.589 --> 00:28:22.195
I thought you were gay.

231
00:28:39.810 --> 00:28:42.051
I can't concentrate.

232
00:28:44.147 --> 00:28:45.592
Maybe another time

233
00:28:55.258 --> 00:28:56.737
Alright.

234
00:28:56.860 --> 00:29:00.330
- Alright, bye.
- And what do we do? Should I call you?

235
00:29:00.464 --> 00:29:04.241
- I'll be back tomorrow.
- Alright. Everything okay?

236
00:29:04.367 --> 00:29:07.314
More or less. Bye.

237
00:29:23.019 --> 00:29:26.193
Want to eat dinner?
Or should I wait until spring?

238
00:29:26.323 --> 00:29:28.963
- Now?
- Do you have time?

239
00:29:29.993 --> 00:29:34.305
I wanted to start swimming.
I'm tense.

240
00:29:34.431 --> 00:29:39.676
- It's not time to start swimming.
- No, it's never time to swim.

241
00:29:39.803 --> 00:29:41.407
Then let's eat.

242
00:29:42.105 --> 00:29:44.085
Here it is.

243
00:29:44.241 --> 00:29:46.152
Have you been here?

244
00:29:46.276 --> 00:29:49.723
I've seen the building
but didn't know it has a restaurant.

245
00:29:50.413 --> 00:29:53.292
I thought they were offices.

246
00:29:59.322 --> 00:30:02.633
I thought it might be interesting
for an architect.

247
00:30:18.041 --> 00:30:20.078
Shall we go up?

248
00:30:21.311 --> 00:30:23.120
I'll take the stairs.

249
00:30:23.713 --> 00:30:26.455
It's on the 20th floor.

250
00:30:27.951 --> 00:30:29.624
Well...

251
00:30:31.421 --> 00:30:34.265
You'll probably think I'm crazy,
but...

252
00:30:34.491 --> 00:30:39.338
I have a phobia of them.
I don't use lifts.

253
00:30:39.663 --> 00:30:42.940
Especially when
they're hermetically sealed.

254
00:30:43.066 --> 00:30:45.444
I get claustrophobia.

255
00:30:46.903 --> 00:30:48.507
Do you live on the ground floor?

256
00:30:49.139 --> 00:30:51.483
- On the eighth.
- Seriously?

257
00:30:51.842 --> 00:30:54.914
I think twice before leaving
the flat.

258
00:30:55.178 --> 00:30:58.284
I go up at least three times a day.

259
00:30:59.950 --> 00:31:01.623
Want to try it with me?

260
00:31:02.786 --> 00:31:04.424
What?

261
00:31:04.554 --> 00:31:07.398
No, I mean take the lift.

262
00:31:10.026 --> 00:31:13.235
I know a good
ground floor restaurant

263
00:31:16.666 --> 00:31:21.172
I'm curious to see the city
from so high up.

264
00:31:21.972 --> 00:31:23.713
I'll Walk.

265
00:31:28.178 --> 00:31:31.352
- I'll join you.
- That's not necessary. Really

266
00:31:31.514 --> 00:31:34.723
You wouldn't make it anyway.

267
00:31:34.885 --> 00:31:37.456
- Of course I will.
- But...

268
00:31:37.587 --> 00:31:40.500
Now that you've finally said "yes'"

269
00:31:41.992 --> 00:31:44.233
When did you last use a lift?

270
00:31:44.561 --> 00:31:48.202
- Two or three years ago.
- Did something bad happen?

271
00:31:49.399 --> 00:31:53.279
Don't speak.
You'll be out of breath.

272
00:31:53.570 --> 00:31:56.141
Administer it.

273
00:31:56.973 --> 00:32:01.115
- Have you tried closing your eyes?
- If that would only work.

274
00:32:03.413 --> 00:32:06.860
The last time I climbed so many steps
was at Teotihuacan's Pyramid

275
00:32:07.584 --> 00:32:11.498
of the Sun. 40 degrees

276
00:32:11.655 --> 00:32:15.000
and not one miserable cloud
in the middle of the desert.

277
00:32:15.158 --> 00:32:17.900
And 260 awkward steps.

278
00:32:18.228 --> 00:32:21.675
They say the top of the pyramid
is a point

279
00:32:21.831 --> 00:32:26.280
of cosmic energy
that fulfils your wishes.

280
00:32:27.304 --> 00:32:29.784
My wish was not to fall
on my way back down.

281
00:32:29.906 --> 00:32:35.515
Some people go back down on
their arses so they don't fall.

282
00:32:35.779 --> 00:32:39.158
That's typical of religions:
They promise the improbable

283
00:32:39.282 --> 00:32:42.456
so you don't regret
the sacrifices they demand.

284
00:32:43.053 --> 00:32:46.296
I hope I won't regret
climbing these stairs.

285
00:32:49.059 --> 00:32:51.437
Everything alright?

286
00:33:05.342 --> 00:33:07.788
Don't you want to take the lift?

287
00:33:08.912 --> 00:33:13.759
- Yes. That'd be better.
- See you at the top.

288
00:33:13.917 --> 00:33:16.488
Smoking is killing me.

289
00:33:17.487 --> 00:33:20.764
- Should I order a water for you?
- Go ahead.

290
00:33:20.890 --> 00:33:24.360
- Sparkling or still?
- Still!

291
00:36:09.325 --> 00:36:13.796
<i>My relationship with Pablo
corresponded with the digital age.</i>

292
00:36:13.930 --> 00:36:15.170
<i>At the beginning,</i>

293
00:36:15.298 --> 00:36:18.507
I bought a camera
that documented those four years.

294
00:36:18.868 --> 00:36:21.348
<i>380 photographs the first year.</i>

295
00:36:21.504 --> 00:36:25.680
<i>176 in the second.
97 in the third.</i>

296
00:36:25.808 --> 00:36:28.516
<i>And four in the last year.</i>

297
00:36:35.485 --> 00:36:38.227
<i>In a simple, irreversible act,</i>

298
00:36:38.354 --> 00:36:42.325
<i>I'm doing away
with 38.9 megabytes of history.</i>

299
00:36:44.160 --> 00:36:47.573
<i>If only my head worked as well
as my Mac</i>

300
00:36:47.697 --> 00:36:50.678
<i>and I could forget everything
with a click.</i>

301
00:36:54.737 --> 00:36:59.914
<i>Is there anything more discouraging
in the 21st century as an empty in-box?</i>

302
00:37:00.743 --> 00:37:03.519
<i>Luckily, a friend wrote to me
from Oman.</i>

303
00:37:03.646 --> 00:37:06.786
<i>He happens to be visiting Yemen</i>

304
00:37:06.916 --> 00:37:11.160
<i>and needs my help withdrawing
9.5 million dollars</i>

305
00:37:11.287 --> 00:37:15.030
<i>from a local bank.
He hasn't written for a while.</i>

306
00:37:15.158 --> 00:37:18.105
<i>It's an imprudent,
one-sided friendship.</i>

307
00:37:24.701 --> 00:37:27.705
Please give us some space.

308
00:37:47.223 --> 00:37:49.260
<i>Before I had my lift phobia,</i>

309
00:37:49.392 --> 00:37:52.168
<i>I gave tours
in the Kavanagh building.</i>

310
00:37:53.129 --> 00:37:56.110
<i>I had no problem taking
the 15 lifts</i>

311
00:37:56.232 --> 00:38:00.112
<i>through 31 floors of
the city's most beautiful skyscraper:</i>

312
00:38:00.236 --> 00:38:04.810
<i>The world's largest concrete
construction at the end of the 1930s.</i>

313
00:38:05.475 --> 00:38:09.287
<i>A building as striking
as the history that it conceals.</i>

314
00:38:10.480 --> 00:38:12.983
<i>Corina Kavanagh
was a beautiful woman</i>

315
00:38:13.116 --> 00:38:16.188
<i>from a wealthy,
but plebeian family</i>

316
00:38:16.319 --> 00:38:19.960
<i>who fell in love
with a young man of nobility.</i>

317
00:38:20.089 --> 00:38:24.333
<i>The Anchorenas opposed
the relationship and broke it up.</i>

318
00:38:24.460 --> 00:38:26.337
<i>Their most important possession</i>

319
00:38:26.462 --> 00:38:29.033
<i>was the Basilica
Santisimo Sacramento,</i>

320
00:38:29.332 --> 00:38:33.747
<i>a temple they built
which later became the family tomb.</i>

321
00:38:35.271 --> 00:38:38.775
<i>The Anchorenas' palace
was on the other side of the park</i>

322
00:38:38.908 --> 00:38:42.378
<i>and they wanted a new one
next to the basilica.</i>

323
00:38:42.578 --> 00:38:44.717
<i>Corina Kavanagh sold three farms</i>

324
00:38:44.847 --> 00:38:48.818
<i>and had a skyscraper built
for one reason:</i>

325
00:38:48.951 --> 00:38:50.988
<i>To hide the basilica's facade</i>

326
00:38:51.120 --> 00:38:54.761
<i>and prevent the Anchorenas
from seeing it.</i>

327
00:38:54.891 --> 00:38:58.429
<i>It's only visible
from "Corina Kavanagh" alley.</i>

328
00:39:01.130 --> 00:39:04.304
<i>When I have a daughter,
I'll name her Corina.</i>

329
00:39:56.552 --> 00:40:00.625
HOW WAS YOUR DAY?

330
00:41:01.784 --> 00:41:03.320
Great, thanks. Here!

331
00:41:06.355 --> 00:41:08.767
WHERE ARE YOU?

332
00:41:24.941 --> 00:41:28.150
I'M AT THE CINEMA

333
00:41:40.423 --> 00:41:43.961
- Can I put some music on?
- Of course.

334
00:41:50.399 --> 00:41:53.403
WHY DIDN'T YOU TELL ME.
WHAT MOVIE?

335
00:41:58.274 --> 00:42:01.551
You have thousands.
How am I supposed to choose?

336
00:42:07.016 --> 00:42:11.431
- I don't like them.
- Let me choose one.

337
00:42:31.908 --> 00:42:35.014
I WANT TO SEE YOU.
WILL MEET YOU AT THE EXIT.

338
00:42:40.650 --> 00:42:43.631
IT'S TERRIBLY LONG.
I'M TURNING MY MOBILE OFF.

339
00:42:43.753 --> 00:42:45.562
SENDING TO MARIELA

340
00:42:55.264 --> 00:42:58.802
- You hardly ate anything.
- I don't eat much in the evening.

341
00:43:13.049 --> 00:43:16.053
Did I tell you I'm writing a play?

342
00:43:17.219 --> 00:43:19.631
You've hardly said anything all night.

343
00:43:23.059 --> 00:43:27.269
Really? I'm in a weird mood,
I don't know why.

344
00:43:28.564 --> 00:43:30.339
I didn't know you write

345
00:43:30.967 --> 00:43:33.208
Me neither.

346
00:43:33.769 --> 00:43:35.749
Until now.

347
00:43:36.405 --> 00:43:38.407
I've studied theatre for a while.

348
00:43:42.845 --> 00:43:44.950
Should I tell you about it?

349
00:43:46.415 --> 00:43:48.827
It's called "Cruel Woman".

350
00:43:49.218 --> 00:43:51.664
There's a swing on stage,

351
00:43:51.787 --> 00:43:54.165
the protagonist
and seven Dalmatians.

352
00:43:55.391 --> 00:43:59.931
So the audience listens to
a monologue with headphones,

353
00:44:00.062 --> 00:44:03.703
and what they hear are
her thoughts.

354
00:44:04.066 --> 00:44:05.909
She's planning

355
00:44:06.068 --> 00:44:09.811
to kill the dogs' owners
so she can keep them for herself.

356
00:44:12.108 --> 00:44:14.554
You like it?

357
00:44:17.313 --> 00:44:21.227
It sounds good.
I'd have to read it.

358
00:44:22.685 --> 00:44:25.256
I act in it.

359
00:44:25.888 --> 00:44:28.232
You're the one who
walks the drugs?

360
00:44:28.357 --> 00:44:31.668
- "Walks the drugs"?
- Did I say "drugs"?

361
00:44:33.996 --> 00:44:35.669
Good one!

362
00:45:59.615 --> 00:46:01.094
11 MESSAGES

363
00:46:08.724 --> 00:46:11.466
I'M GOING HOME.

364
00:46:23.572 --> 00:46:27.418
Don't get any false hopes.
It was just sex.

365
00:46:53.536 --> 00:46:57.313
<i>They sprout out of the cement,
growing where they shouldn't.</i>

366
00:46:58.307 --> 00:47:02.585
<i>Rising up patiently
with exemplary will and dignity.</i>

367
00:47:03.445 --> 00:47:08.155
<i>Without lineage,
wild, unclassifiable by botanists.</i>

368
00:47:08.350 --> 00:47:11.229
<i>A strange, rampant,
absurd beauty.</i>

369
00:47:12.922 --> 00:47:15.198
<i>They adorn the greyest of corners,</i>

370
00:47:15.624 --> 00:47:18.537
<i>don't own anything
and nothing can stop them.</i>

371
00:47:18.994 --> 00:47:21.235
<i>A metaphor for uncontrollable life</i>

372
00:47:21.363 --> 00:47:24.970
<i>that paradoxically forces me
to face my weakness.</i>

373
00:50:18.507 --> 00:50:23.422
<i>I'm Marcella, a psychologist,
and my job is my passion.</i>

374
00:50:24.113 --> 00:50:27.390
<i>Calm down, I won't analyze you.</i>

375
00:50:27.516 --> 00:50:31.191
<i>As a rule,
I don't analyze friends.</i>

376
00:50:31.320 --> 00:50:34.858
<i>To be honest, I'm very selective
regarding friendships.</i>

377
00:50:34.990 --> 00:50:38.995
<i>I write a lot
and love self-help books.</i>

378
00:50:39.228 --> 00:50:41.731
<i>Gender: female. Age: 31.</i>

379
00:50:41.864 --> 00:50:46.313
<i>Marital status: single.
Children: none. Religion: Buddhist.</i>

380
00:50:46.435 --> 00:50:50.383
<i>Not a drinker. Occasional smoker.
University degree.</i>

381
00:50:50.506 --> 00:50:53.851
<i>Languages: French, English,
German, Italian,</i>

382
00:50:53.976 --> 00:50:56.513
<i>Portuguese, Greek,
Spanish, of course,</i>

383
00:50:56.645 --> 00:50:58.454
<i>and a bit of Russian.</i>

384
00:50:58.814 --> 00:51:03.285
<i>Hobbies: alternative music,
jazz, blues, Latin American,</i>

385
00:51:03.419 --> 00:51:05.763
disco, 80's, electronic,

386
00:51:05.921 --> 00:51:08.424
<i>pop, rhythm and blues,
rock, classical,</i>

387
00:51:08.557 --> 00:51:12.562
<i>theatre, ballet, travelling,
yoga, meditation,</i>

388
00:51:12.694 --> 00:51:15.834
<i>movies, shoes, cars, motorcycles,
working out,</i>

389
00:51:15.998 --> 00:51:19.070
<i>writing, cooking, Internet,
water sports,</i>

390
00:51:19.201 --> 00:51:22.478
<i>nature, politics, charity,
wine tasting,</i>

391
00:51:22.604 --> 00:51:25.278
<i>mountaineering, shopping,
tarot, an,</i>

392
00:51:25.407 --> 00:51:27.785
martial arts, tai chi chuan,
I Ching,

393
00:51:27.910 --> 00:51:30.390
<i>feng shui, astrology, New Age.</i>

394
00:51:49.198 --> 00:51:54.238
No, I'll listen, but I don't speak it.
I understand everything, but...

395
00:51:55.003 --> 00:51:56.038
I don't speak it.

396
00:52:02.711 --> 00:52:06.523
No, seriously, I speak it poorly.
I don't like hearing myself.

397
00:52:06.815 --> 00:52:08.522
I have no control.

398
00:52:08.650 --> 00:52:10.527
I say what I can instead of what
I want to.

399
00:52:19.895 --> 00:52:21.602
Well, you speak it.

400
00:52:25.467 --> 00:52:27.743
Your French sounds French.

401
00:52:34.409 --> 00:52:37.913
<i>These dates are like
McDonalds combos.</i>

402
00:52:39.915 --> 00:52:43.692
<i>They look larger and more delicious
in the photographs.</i>

403
00:52:45.287 --> 00:52:46.960
<i>Every time I go on a date,</i>

404
00:52:47.089 --> 00:52:50.798
<i>I suffer from the same deception
as with a Big Mac.</i>

405
00:53:08.377 --> 00:53:10.288
No, nothing at all.

406
00:53:31.400 --> 00:53:33.505
No more, thank you.

407
00:53:35.337 --> 00:53:37.715
What were you doing alone in Paris?

408
00:53:38.207 --> 00:53:41.677
Oh no, that's a long story.

409
00:53:42.444 --> 00:53:44.890
But I can tell you how it ends.

410
00:53:45.013 --> 00:53:47.050
We separated at De Gaulle.

411
00:53:47.216 --> 00:53:51.392
Yes, a 13-hour flight
is too much for a couple in crisis

412
00:53:51.520 --> 00:53:54.296
Luckily, we both had
our own luggage.

413
00:53:54.423 --> 00:53:58.530
We divided up our goods
in the airport.

414
00:53:58.861 --> 00:54:02.172
He kept the shampoo,
I kept the conditioner...

415
00:54:02.364 --> 00:54:05.811
We split up the few things
we had in common.

416
00:54:06.401 --> 00:54:09.871
He went to Berlin,
I stayed in Paris.

417
00:54:10.339 --> 00:54:14.481
I cried for 24 hours
and that was it.

418
00:54:22.651 --> 00:54:24.187
Everything alright?

419
00:54:24.319 --> 00:54:26.629
Should I buy you a water?

420
00:54:29.057 --> 00:54:31.230
Nervous ness.

421
00:54:32.761 --> 00:54:34.798
I'm nervous.

422
00:54:35.764 --> 00:54:37.801
I need movement.

423
00:54:38.934 --> 00:54:40.106
Want to dance?

424
00:54:40.335 --> 00:54:43.873
I have a great need to communicate.

425
00:54:44.473 --> 00:54:49.081
For example,
if we'd met here by chance

426
00:54:49.211 --> 00:54:52.556
and you only spoke Italian...

427
00:54:52.681 --> 00:54:57.357
But let's say
I didn't understand it.

428
00:54:57.786 --> 00:55:02.201
I would have lost the opportunity
to meet a very beautiful person.

429
00:55:36.258 --> 00:55:37.635
What did you say?

430
00:55:39.061 --> 00:55:40.802
Look it up.

431
00:55:49.471 --> 00:55:51.348
Bloody bastard!

432
00:55:52.874 --> 00:55:55.184
How can you be so stupid?

433
00:55:55.744 --> 00:55:58.987
Bye, Susْ. Come here,
I'm late. Bye.

434
00:56:09.925 --> 00:56:12.496
<i>This is the 15th time
I've signed up for swimming</i>

435
00:56:12.628 --> 00:56:15.575
<i>and the 15th time I won't go.</i>

436
00:56:15.831 --> 00:56:20.177
<i>I love swimming, but I hate
everything before and after it.</i>

437
00:56:20.335 --> 00:56:22.941
<i>I hate showering before
and after.</i>

438
00:56:23.071 --> 00:56:25.915
<i>I hate getting undressed
and dressed,</i>

439
00:56:26.041 --> 00:56:27.679
<i>drying my hair in the winter,</i>

440
00:56:27.809 --> 00:56:30.221
<i>a wet towel in my bag,</i>

441
00:56:30.345 --> 00:56:33.383
<i>a wet swimsuit, wet flip-flops...</i>

442
00:56:33.749 --> 00:56:35.820
<i>I hate the smell of chlorine.</i>

443
00:56:35.951 --> 00:56:39.194
<i>I hate the membership card
and the medical examination:</i>

444
00:56:39.321 --> 00:56:44.464
<i>Having to show someone
what's between my toes.</i>

445
00:56:44.660 --> 00:56:47.504
<i>I hate that people
piss in the water.</i>

446
00:56:48.096 --> 00:56:52.010
<i>I hate caps and the amazed
expression goggles give you.</i>

447
00:56:52.367 --> 00:56:57.817
<i>And what I hate most is the fact that
it's the most complete sport there is.</i>

448
00:57:18.126 --> 00:57:20.037
Tired?

449
00:57:23.532 --> 00:57:25.603
Are you tired?

450
00:57:28.503 --> 00:57:30.915
You have to kick less.

451
00:57:31.239 --> 00:57:34.584
Legs consume more oxygen
than arms.

452
00:57:34.710 --> 00:57:38.021
Kicking is more for stabilization
than for propulsion.

453
00:57:38.146 --> 00:57:40.922
Stretch out and relax your legs.

454
00:57:41.183 --> 00:57:43.629
When your hand goes
into the water,

455
00:57:43.752 --> 00:57:47.632
stretch it out, feel the impulse
and the other arm follows.

456
00:57:48.090 --> 00:57:49.899
Your hands under your body.

457
00:57:50.025 --> 00:57:53.973
And lower your head. The water
should be up to your forehead.

458
00:57:54.496 --> 00:57:57.204
You're doing 32 strokes.
Try what I'm saying.

459
00:57:57.332 --> 00:57:59.972
You'll do fewer strokes
and tire less.

460
00:58:00.102 --> 00:58:02.173
You'll swim in a more relaxed way.

461
00:58:02.304 --> 00:58:06.946
And try breathing every third stroke.
Go ahead, I'll count.

462
00:58:14.549 --> 00:58:16.859
- May I?
- Yes.

463
00:58:51.052 --> 00:58:53.726
- And?
- You were right, I'm not as tired.

464
00:58:53.855 --> 00:58:57.325
30 strokes. Two fewer per lap
and you can get even lower.

465
00:58:57.459 --> 00:59:01.202
- Are you a teacher?
- I'm a compulsive swimmer.

466
00:59:01.363 --> 00:59:04.503
I can't sleep if
I don't swim an hour a day.

467
00:59:04.633 --> 00:59:07.477
Really? Exhaustion from swimming
is unrivalled.

468
00:59:07.636 --> 00:59:10.207
Yes, nothing else tires me out.

469
00:59:12.140 --> 00:59:14.313
- What's your name?
- Rafa. And you?

470
00:59:14.443 --> 00:59:16.548
Mariana.

471
00:59:18.046 --> 00:59:20.549
What's it, asshole?

472
00:59:20.682 --> 00:59:22.992
- Let me past!
- Go ahead!

473
00:59:23.118 --> 00:59:26.122
- You're a clown!
- What the hell are you doing?

474
00:59:27.022 --> 00:59:28.228
Idiot!

475
00:59:28.390 --> 00:59:31.371
Always the same! Screw you!

476
00:59:31.493 --> 00:59:34.440
Don't touch me! Don't touch me!

477
00:59:35.096 --> 00:59:39.943
I still have 30 laps!
I'm a member and I paid my fee!

478
00:59:40.202 --> 00:59:45.982
- This is supposed to relax you.
- Living healthily is stressful.

479
00:59:48.043 --> 00:59:51.422
Being conscious of everything
only to live a bit longer

480
00:59:51.546 --> 00:59:54.049
is the worst thing we can do.

481
00:59:55.050 --> 00:59:59.624
None of us come here for fun
We come to count laps.

482
01:00:00.655 --> 01:00:04.694
- Why can't you sleep?
- I thought you'd know already.

483
01:00:04.826 --> 01:00:08.364
There's no switch
to turn off my head.

484
01:00:08.497 --> 01:00:11.569
- Have you seen a psychologist?
- Yes, I am a psychologist.

485
01:00:13.768 --> 01:00:16.681
So swimming is the only thing
that works?

486
01:00:16.805 --> 01:00:20.048
If I could,
I'd swim to my bedside.

487
01:00:25.614 --> 01:00:28.288
'28! .very good!

488
01:00:33.622 --> 01:00:36.068
Tomorrow I can't swim

489
01:00:36.825 --> 01:00:40.705
I've got patients until 10 p.m.,
so I won't be able to sleep.

490
01:00:41.830 --> 01:00:44.674
We could go and eat
or drink something.

491
01:00:45.667 --> 01:00:49.672
- Shalli put you to sleep?
- On the contrary.

492
01:00:49.804 --> 01:00:52.944
What a relief. Then yes.

493
01:00:54.376 --> 01:00:58.449
Let's take off our caps and goggles
so we'll recognise each other.

494
01:00:58.580 --> 01:01:00.651
No, I'll come like this.

495
01:02:26.468 --> 01:02:28.914
It doesn't matter, I don't care.

496
01:02:33.642 --> 01:02:35.747
Everything's alright.

497
01:06:14.495 --> 01:06:16.736
<i>The brilliant morning light</i>

498
01:06:16.865 --> 01:06:19.778
<i>let me see the reflection clearly.</i>

499
01:06:21.536 --> 01:06:27.384
<i>Late, as always,
I realised it was me in the window.</i>

500
01:06:27.508 --> 01:06:29.317
<i>Like a mannequin.</i>

501
01:06:29.444 --> 01:06:33.551
<i>Motionless, silent and cold.</i>

502
01:06:45.426 --> 01:06:48.703
SPRING AT LAST

503
01:06:59.274 --> 01:07:03.723
<i>Absolutely all of the buildings
have a useless, purposeless side.</i>

504
01:07:04.312 --> 01:07:09.386
<i>It's neither the front nor the back:</i>

505
01:07:09.684 --> 01:07:11.129
<i>The ”medianera” or "side wall“.</i>

506
01:07:11.252 --> 01:07:15.064
<i>Enormous surfaces that divide us,
reminding us of passing time,</i>

507
01:07:15.189 --> 01:07:17.191
<i>smog and filth.</i>

508
01:07:24.866 --> 01:07:27.779
<i>”Medianeras”
show our worst attributes.</i>

509
01:07:28.937 --> 01:07:32.180
They reflect inconstancy, cracks,

510
01:07:32.473 --> 01:07:34.851
<i>provisional solutions,</i>

511
01:07:34.976 --> 01:07:38.048
<i>and the dirt we sweep under
the carpet.</i>

512
01:07:38.246 --> 01:07:41.159
<i>We only remember
them in exceptional cases</i>

513
01:07:41.482 --> 01:07:43.894
<i>when, during bad weather,</i>

514
01:07:44.018 --> 01:07:45.622
<i>its ads filter through.</i>

515
01:07:47.922 --> 01:07:51.335
<i>”Medianeras” have changed
into another medium for ads</i>

516
01:07:51.459 --> 01:07:54.599
<i>that are seldom beautiful.</i>

517
01:07:56.264 --> 01:07:59.302
<i>Generally,
they indicate how far we are</i>

518
01:07:59.434 --> 01:08:03.314
<i>from supermarkets and fast food.</i>

519
01:08:03.805 --> 01:08:08.083
<i>Lottery ads that promise a lot
for little.</i>

520
01:08:11.479 --> 01:08:12.685
AVAILABLE

521
01:08:14.315 --> 01:08:18.889
<i>Recently, they've reminded us of
the economic crisis that left us</i>

522
01:08:19.253 --> 01:08:21.358
<i>unemployed.</i>

523
01:08:22.557 --> 01:08:26.266
<i>There's one way out of the oppression
that results from living in a shoebox.</i>

524
01:08:26.694 --> 01:08:29.197
<i>An escape route:</i>

525
01:08:29.897 --> 01:08:32.241
<i>Illegal, like all escape routes.</i>

526
01:08:33.234 --> 01:08:37.649
<i>In clear violation
of urban planning norms,</i>

527
01:08:37.772 --> 01:08:42.312
<i>there are a few tiny,
irregular, irresponsible windows</i>

528
01:08:42.443 --> 01:08:45.424
<i>that let a few miraculous
rays of light</i>

529
01:08:45.546 --> 01:08:48.220
<i>into our darkness.</i>

530
01:09:19.013 --> 01:09:21.050
May I?

531
01:13:47.048 --> 01:13:49.654
<i>When will we be a wireless city?</i>

532
01:13:50.384 --> 01:13:55.697
<i>What geniuses blocked the river
with buildings and the sky with wires?</i>

533
01:13:57.525 --> 01:14:00.972
<i>Are all those kilometers
of wire there to unite us</i>

534
01:14:01.262 --> 01:14:05.369
<i>or to divide us?
Everyone in his own place.</i>

535
01:14:05.499 --> 01:14:10.312
<i>Cellular telephony invaded the world
promising to always keep us connected.</i>

536
01:14:10.838 --> 01:14:12.840
<i>Text messaging:</i>

537
01:14:12.973 --> 01:14:15.817
<i>A new ten-key system</i>

538
01:14:15.943 --> 01:14:18.446
<i>that reduces one of the most
beautiful languages</i>

539
01:14:18.579 --> 01:14:22.425
<i>to a primitive,
limited and guttural vocabulary.</i>

540
01:14:22.983 --> 01:14:26.760
<i>The future is in fibre optics,
say the visionaries.</i>

541
01:14:27.388 --> 01:14:32.269
<i>They've announced that we'll be able
to heat our homes from our workplaces.</i>

542
01:14:32.393 --> 01:14:37.069
<i>Right! And no one will be waiting
for us when we get home.</i>

543
01:14:37.998 --> 01:14:40.842
<i>Welcome to the era
of virtual relationships.</i>

544
01:14:45.339 --> 01:14:47.444
Is this your first time chatting?

545
01:14:50.578 --> 01:14:52.615
Yes. Did you notice?

546
01:14:53.180 --> 01:14:56.923
Very much so.
You have to begin with M or W.

547
01:14:57.184 --> 01:14:58.720
What's that?

548
01:15:01.956 --> 01:15:03.731
Man or woman

549
01:15:03.958 --> 01:15:07.337
Woman. A broad term, isn't it?

550
01:15:08.129 --> 01:15:10.973
- And you?
- Man.

551
01:15:12.733 --> 01:15:16.613
- Do I have to believe you?
- It's easy to verify.

552
01:15:16.737 --> 01:15:20.207
I write like a man,
think like a man,

553
01:15:20.474 --> 01:15:22.647
and act like a man.

554
01:15:26.614 --> 01:15:28.355
And?

555
01:15:29.483 --> 01:15:31.520
What?

556
01:15:32.853 --> 01:15:36.062
Your turn. In the arse

557
01:15:39.460 --> 01:15:41.599
I don't know what to say.

558
01:15:41.829 --> 01:15:45.106
It's strange talking to someone
I don't know.

559
01:15:46.467 --> 01:15:48.640
What do you want to know?

560
01:15:50.905 --> 01:15:53.408
I don't even know where to begin

561
01:15:54.175 --> 01:15:56.451
I'm taking off. Bye.

562
01:15:57.044 --> 01:16:00.890
Don't leave! Wait!
Are you still there?

563
01:16:08.322 --> 01:16:10.768
I'll give you a list of questions:

564
01:16:10.891 --> 01:16:13.838
How old are you?
What sign are you?

565
01:16:13.994 --> 01:16:16.668
What music do you listen to?
Do you believe in God?

566
01:16:18.032 --> 01:16:19.909
What did you do today?

567
01:16:22.503 --> 01:16:24.710
That's cheating.

568
01:16:27.508 --> 01:16:29.317
It's a question.

569
01:16:31.111 --> 01:16:34.558
Okay. I woke up at twelve
because I went to bed at five.

570
01:16:34.682 --> 01:16:37.720
I should start swimming.
I ate breakfast at one.

571
01:16:37.918 --> 01:16:39.898
I took an ibuprofen.

572
01:16:40.154 --> 01:16:43.328
At two I started work.
I do web design.

573
01:16:43.657 --> 01:16:47.195
At five I ate lunch.
At eight I went to therapy.

574
01:16:47.528 --> 01:16:49.405
I took my second ibuprofen.

575
01:16:49.530 --> 01:16:51.669
Then the telephone rang,
which made me happy.

576
01:16:51.866 --> 01:16:54.938
Wrong number.
Now I'm eating dinner.

577
01:16:55.135 --> 01:16:58.116
After chatting, I'll whack myself on
the head so I can fall asleep

578
01:16:58.272 --> 01:17:00.309
and start swimming tomorrow.

579
01:17:05.913 --> 01:17:07.688
DID YOU TAKE OFF?

580
01:17:09.450 --> 01:17:10.690
YES!

581
01:17:14.355 --> 01:17:17.529
Good. What did you do today?

582
01:17:18.926 --> 01:17:22.999
Adaptation.
Getting used to being single again.

583
01:17:26.433 --> 01:17:29.380
I'm an expert.
I could write a book.

584
01:17:29.537 --> 01:17:32.074
I'm a kind of depressed
Paulo Coelho.

585
01:17:35.442 --> 01:17:37.012
I didn't have a good day.

586
01:17:37.378 --> 01:17:39.187
I'M SO SAD.

587
01:17:41.282 --> 01:17:44.525
I have a method.
Absolutely involuntary.

588
01:17:44.852 --> 01:17:49.096
A kind of Buddhist gene that makes
my happy days not so happy

589
01:17:49.223 --> 01:17:51.294
and my sad days not so sad.

590
01:17:54.995 --> 01:17:57.669
A spiritual thermostat.

591
01:18:05.606 --> 01:18:07.313
And if it fails?

592
01:18:09.043 --> 01:18:10.545
I down a Rivotril.

593
01:18:13.414 --> 01:18:15.121
I didn't think I'd laugh today.

594
01:18:17.818 --> 01:18:21.891
In return, do me a favour.
When do you wake up?

595
01:18:24.592 --> 01:18:26.469
Nine.

596
01:18:26.594 --> 01:18:30.474
I'll give you my number. Call me
at nine and motivate me to swim.

597
01:18:32.299 --> 01:18:34.370
Why not now?

598
01:18:34.835 --> 01:18:37.509
No, that won't do. It's a deal.

599
01:18:39.673 --> 01:18:41.653
Give me your number.

600
01:18:49.950 --> 01:18:54.160
Bloody bastard! Fucking hell!

601
01:19:04.098 --> 01:19:05.907
Bloody bastard!

602
01:19:12.039 --> 01:19:16.215
Why do I keep all this shit?

603
01:19:18.345 --> 01:19:20.347
Wait Susْ, I'll be right back.

604
01:19:38.432 --> 01:19:42.141
- Do you have any candles?
- Yes, wait a minute...

605
01:19:42.302 --> 01:19:44.441
I'll be right with you.

606
01:19:50.010 --> 01:19:51.956
Four pesos.

607
01:19:57.751 --> 01:20:02.222
- Any candles left?
- I think so. Let me look...

608
01:20:12.666 --> 01:20:16.204
- I got a shock.
- Yeah, me too.

609
01:20:16.603 --> 01:20:20.949
- Probably the flashlight.
- I don't know, but it was heavy.

610
01:20:30.451 --> 01:20:34.456
Here they are, the last ones.
Your lucky day!

611
01:20:34.588 --> 01:20:37.000
Right, good to know.

612
01:20:37.124 --> 01:20:38.865
- How much?
- Four pesos.

613
01:20:40.794 --> 01:20:43.934
- My luck's a bit expensive.
- Yeah.

614
01:20:52.005 --> 01:20:55.680
- How much for this?
- Nine.

615
01:20:55.876 --> 01:20:58.322
Thanks. Bye.

616
01:21:02.683 --> 01:21:04.094
Something else?

617
01:21:05.152 --> 01:21:07.758
What do you have for a peso
so we can round it up?
